"""Class 10 SST Ch 10 Minerals and Energy Resources (Geography) - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Magnetite is the finest iron ore.", r"Magnetite has an iron content of up to 70 per cent and excellent magnetic qualities.", "a",
     r"The very high iron content makes it the finest ore; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Manganese is mainly used in the manufacturing of steel and ferro-manganese alloys.", r"It is also used in making bleaching powder, insecticides and paints.", "b",
     r"Both true uses of manganese, but R is an additional use, not an explanation."),
 AR4(r"Lignite is a low-grade brown coal that is soft with high moisture content.", r"Lignite has the highest carbon content of all types of coal.", "c",
     r"A is true. Anthracite has the highest carbon content, so R is false."),
 AR4(r"Hydroelectricity is a non-renewable source of energy.", r"Hydel power is generated from the flow of water.", "d",
     r"Flowing water is replenished by the hydrological cycle, so hydel power is renewable; A is false, R is true."),
 AR4(r"Placer deposits contain minerals such as gold, silver, tin and platinum.", r"Placer deposits occur as alluvial deposits in sands of valley floors and base of hills, and the minerals are not corroded by water.", "a",
     r"These minerals resist corrosion and so accumulate in sand deposits; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Biogas plants using cattle dung are called Gobar gas plants.", r"Monazite sands of Kerala are rich in thorium.", "b",
     r"Both true statements about energy resources; they are unrelated."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Minerals generally occur in these forms: in igneous and metamorphic rocks they occur in cracks, crevices, faults or joints; smaller occurrences are called veins and larger ones lodes. In sedimentary rocks, minerals occur in beds or layers, as in coal and some forms of iron ore. Certain minerals occur as alluvial deposits in sands of valley floors and are called placer deposits. "
   r"(i) What are the smaller occurrences of minerals in rocks called? (ii) Name a mineral formed in sedimentary rocks. (iii) What are placer deposits? (iv) Why must we use minerals carefully?",
   r"(i) Veins (1). (ii) Coal (or some iron ore) (1). (iii) Alluvial deposits of minerals in sands of valley floors and hill bases (1). (iv) Mineral resources are finite and non-renewable; they take millions of years to form, so conservation is essential (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In a village in Uttar Pradesh, a farmer set up a plant that uses cattle dung and farm waste to produce gas for cooking. Decomposition of organic matter yields gas which has higher thermal efficiency than kerosene, dung cake and charcoal. The plant also gives improved quality manure for his fields. Plants using cattle dung are known as 'Gobar gas plants'. "
   r"(i) What is the energy resource produced in the plant? (ii) Is it conventional or non-conventional? (iii) Name any one of its two benefits to the farmer. (iv) Why is biogas more suitable for rural India than dung cakes or firewood?",
   r"(i) Biogas (1). (ii) Non-conventional (1). (iii) Energy for cooking or improved manure (1). (iv) Burning dung cakes and firewood wastes manure and reduces forests, whereas biogas is clean, efficient and still leaves manure for the fields (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Coal is the most abundantly available fossil fuel in India. Lignite is a low-grade brown coal with high moisture content, found at Neyveli in Tamil Nadu. Bituminous coal is the most popular coal in commercial use. Anthracite is the highest quality hard coal. Gondwana coal is a little over 200 million years old while Tertiary coal is about 55 million years old. "
   r"(i) Which is the highest quality coal? (ii) Where are the principal lignite reserves? (iii) Which coal is most popular in commercial use? (iv) Why does lignite give less energy than anthracite?",
   r"(i) Anthracite (1). (ii) Neyveli in Tamil Nadu (1). (iii) Bituminous coal (1). (iv) Lignite has high moisture and low carbon content, hence low heating capacity, while anthracite has high carbon content (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

